    <qual:listOfQualitativeSpecies xmlns:qual="http://www.sbml.org/sbml/level3/version1/qual/version1">
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="f031f1d3-dbd0-40cf-b420-999c1a97e836" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_1" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="GLI2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Glioma-associated oncogene family members (Gli1/2/3) act as a transcription factor downtream of hedgehog signalling in skeletal cells</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16571352</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26023726</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">MGI:95728 , NCBI gene:14633</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#f031f1d3-dbd0-40cf-b420-999c1a97e836">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="a6160af2-544b-4a56-a6a0-866ede935046" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_2" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="BMP">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">The downstream effect of BMP ligands depends on the receptors they bind to. The model only includes a subset of potential  downstream effects (noth canonical and non canonical)</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Overexpression of BMP2 was found in osteoarthritic tissues</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12681954</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20211748</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25401122</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">In growth plate BMP2 and 6 are not expressed in proliferative chondrocytes and the level rises till hypetrophy.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17400805</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Bone Morphogenic proteins are important factors regulating chondrogenesis and skeletogenesis during normal embryonic development</p><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">e.g. Bmp2, MGI:88177; NCBI gene: 12156</p><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">BMP signalling play a important role in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20211748</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#a6160af2-544b-4a56-a6a0-866ede935046">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T10:44:36Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_4fd73e9b-0f96-45e6-86a1-5a6129dfbd6f" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_3" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ATF2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">ATF2 activation, downstream of the p38 and JNK kinase, may be involved in both pro-catabolic and pro-anabolic processes, the exact mechanisms are yet to be discovered.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33188182</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Activating transcription factor 2 (Atf2 or Creb2): MGI:109349 , NCBI gene:11909</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">ATF-2 activation inhibits chondrocyte hypertrophy in mouse articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">ATF2 also called CRE-BP1, CREB2, is a cAMP response element (CRE). It has a transcriptional activity and acts downstream  of the TGF-β  pathway</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">10085140</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_4fd73e9b-0f96-45e6-86a1-5a6129dfbd6f">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-06-25T10:57:16Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_9738758c-7541-4bf6-8e55-0fdca4689809" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_4" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="Proteoglycans">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">They provide to cartilage its load bearing function and biomechanical properties</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">32584809</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15952258</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Proteoglycans are extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins that have been functionalized by some glycans. Aggrecan is the major type of proteoglycan in the articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11942407</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">gene: Aggrecan Acan (or Agc1). MGI:99602, NCBI Gene: 11595</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Proteoglycans are among the main constituents of the ECM, secreted by chondrocytes, they provide to cartilage its hydrogel property, which is essential for the load-bearing function of the knee joint.</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">30813547</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16143826</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Loss of proteoglycans is one of the hallmark of osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">30813547</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Proteoglycans amount to 4-7% by wet weight of cartilage ECM</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29800616</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_6" contentType="Description">Proteglycans give to articulat cartilage its ability to resist compressive loads</p><ul key="P_description_6">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">7919526</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">629741</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_9738758c-7541-4bf6-8e55-0fdca4689809">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T13:14:57Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="bf3af857-96a4-4374-bc2c-c7a94a039e2b" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_5" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="FRZB">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Verapamil (overexpressing sFRP3) protects against cartilage degradation in osteoarthritis by inhibiting Wnt/β-catenin signaling</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24658359</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">FrzB may have a protective role against cartilage degradation and MMP induction in mouse chondrocytes by attenuating deleterious effects of the activation of the canonical Wnt/β-catenin pathway.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24984954</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Frizzled-related protein (Frzb) or secreted frizzled-related protein 3 (sFRP3) : MGI:892032, NCBI gene: 20378</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Frzb mRNA level negatively correlates with osteoarthritis and is regulated by osteoarthritis-associated factors. Frzb is a natural brake on hypertrophic differentiation in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24286177</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18050203</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Frzb deficient mices have reduction of  cell proliferation and differentiation in the articular cartilage - subchondral bone</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22264237</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">"Frizzled related proteins (Frps) are secreted proteins structurally similar to frizzled receptors; they bind Wnt via the cysteine-rich domain and antagonize the Wnt signaling pathway"</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12813463</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#bf3af857-96a4-4374-bc2c-c7a94a039e2b">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="b0155e88-b9ed-4e25-878b-e57d436d0d4b" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_6" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ELK-1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">They are transcription factors acting downstream of MAPK signalling. In particular they are activated by ERK1/2</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">For instance Elk-1 (NCBI Gene: 13712) is part of the Ets familly of transcription factors and transduces signals downtsream of MAPK to the nucleus. We consider Ets1 and Elk1 redundant in this model</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Ets Familly (Elk1) NCBI Gene: 13712 /  (Ets1) MGI:95455, NCBI gene: 23871</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Ets1, the founding member of the Ets transcription factor family is involved in several developmental processes. It also controls expression of cytokine and chemokine genes (Uniprot).</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">7689222</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#b0155e88-b9ed-4e25-878b-e57d436d0d4b">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="c91036da-8762-4338-9b2f-54dbe395a136" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_7" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="MEF2C">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Among the 4 mammal MEF2 genes, MEF2C (myocyte enhancer factor 2C) have been shown to have an important role in endochondral ossification</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17959722</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22421045</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17336904</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25715393</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Myocyte enhancer factor 2C (Mef2c): MGI:99458, NCBI gene:17260</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">MEF2C is expressed in (pre)hypertrophic chondrocyte promote expression, at the transcriptional level, of genes related to hypertrophy such as Col-X</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17336904</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22421045</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">MEF2C operates upstream of Runx2 in chondrocyte hypertrophy and that is it directly or indirectly responsible for its expression.</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17659995</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#c91036da-8762-4338-9b2f-54dbe395a136">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="ca2d8480-74fb-4272-ac73-6bafcd58b30a" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_8" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="PPR">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">PTH/PTHrP receptor (PPR). Membranar receptor expressed by chondroctes and to which PThrP binds. Expression of this receptor will define if a cell is sensitive to PThrP signalling. PPR is expressed in pre-hypertrophic chondrocyte in the GP</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12050144</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15771625</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Pth1r, parathyroid hormone 1 receptor. MGI:97801 , NCBI gene19228</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#ca2d8480-74fb-4272-ac73-6bafcd58b30a">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="e5f9882e-762f-4ce1-a9b6-3846c39d2a50" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_9" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="COL-II">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">The so called "Type II" collagen in cartilage is really a  type II, IX and XI combo of fibers</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18442706</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Type II collagen is one of the major component of extracellular matrix (ECM) surrounding proliferative or stable chondrocytes, it amount to 15–22% of the ECM by wet weight</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29800616</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Type II collagen (col2a1): MGI:88452, NCBI gene:12824</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Collagen is present in the shape of fibers in the pericellular and extracellular cartilage matrix. One of the hallmark of osteoarthritis is the replacement of type II collagen by other types, sucha as type X collagen secreted by hypertophic chondrocytes.</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#e5f9882e-762f-4ce1-a9b6-3846c39d2a50">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T18:06:43Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="fda3d8af-5cf0-4e9c-85b4-f7d639c81141" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_10" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="FGFR3">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">FGFR3 and FGFR1 are the major FGF receptor isoforms expressed in human articular cartilage and the modification of a balance between the two might contribute to OA</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21835001</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22833219</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Fgfr3 is expressed in proliferating and prehypertrophic chondrocytes and functions to inhibit postnatal chondrogenesis</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27052727</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15863035</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">MGI:95524, NCBI gene:14184</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Removal of FGFR3 leads to overgrowth . activating mutations in FGFR3 account for several dysplasias such as achondrodysplasia (dawrfism)</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25558817</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">8630492</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698630</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">FGFR‐3 signaling may have a chondroprotective role during the development of OA and FGFR3 expression is downregulated in articular cartilage from OA patients</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16624844</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27159076</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21835001</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Deletion of Fgfr3 in chondrorcytes leads to osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27041063</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#fda3d8af-5cf0-4e9c-85b4-f7d639c81141">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_060b71a4-2a10-43fa-bdb1-7111bcf3dcb1" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_11" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="FGFR1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">FGFR3 and FGFR1 are the major FGF receptor isoforms expressed in human articular cartilage and the modification of a balance between the two might contribute to OA</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21835001</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22833219</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">FGFR1 is expressed in hypetrophic chondrocytes and tabecular bone</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26220993</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15863035</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">fgfr1 (FGF receptor 1) MGI:95522 , NCBI gene:14182</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">FGFR1 inhibitor protects against cartilage degradation in a murine model of osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27041213</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">30201491</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22833219</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Fgfr1 promotes osteoarthitis in articular cartilage. Loss of Fgfr1 reduces hypertophic differentiation</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29691281</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">FGF2 pro-anabolic effect on cartilage would be transduced by FGFR1</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33285208</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_060b71a4-2a10-43fa-bdb1-7111bcf3dcb1">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-15T15:43:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="d3fc33f8-bb95-4bc6-bbe9-1c2fb557b153" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_12" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="DKK1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">dickkopf 1 homolog (Dkk1). MGI:1329040, NCBI gene: 13380</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">DKK1 mRNA level negatively correlates with osteoarthritis and is regulated by osteoarthritis-associated factors. Dkk1 is a natural brake on hypertrophic differentiation in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24286177</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">dickkopf 1 homolog (DKK1) is a Wnt antagonist</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22576962</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Antagonizes canonical Wnt signaling by inhibiting LRP5/6 interaction with Wnt and by forming a ternary complex with the transmembrane protein KREMEN that promotes internalization of LRP5/6</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#d3fc33f8-bb95-4bc6-bbe9-1c2fb557b153">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="f14209e1-4325-4025-9b1e-251a2021f0fc" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_13" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="R_inflam">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Most pro-inflammatory cytokine activated pathways converge in the activation of the TAK1 kinase. This kinase is able to subsequently activate several pathways including ERK1/2/MAPK and NfkB related pathway. TAK1 unlock NfkB transcriptional activity via inhibtion of IkBa, a NfkB specific inhibitor.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20086235</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Receptor of inflammatory cytokines, e.g: Toll-like receptors for IL-beta or TNA-alpha</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24237425</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Downstream target genes include ECM degrading enzymes (e.g. ADMATS5, MMP13) but gene coding for pro-inflammatory cytokines, which constitute a feedforward loop.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16146751</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11714393</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21788902</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#f14209e1-4325-4025-9b1e-251a2021f0fc">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T19:35:31Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_235c9e4f-d61a-4874-aece-cd0dde59a1bf" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_14" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="COL-X">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Type collagen is one of the main component of extracellular matrix typically secreted by hypertrophic chondrocytes, contributing to cartilage mineralization during osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">30853397</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15667640</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Type X collagen (col10a1): MGI:88445, NCBI gene: 12813</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_235c9e4f-d61a-4874-aece-cd0dde59a1bf">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_1379a120-fc05-458d-82c9-aebacf6fe4be" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_15" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="RAS">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Several growth factors can signal through Ras signalling in a non canonical way, the nature of the receptor involved is not always well defined and we assumed that the receptor activation was lineraly correlated to Ras activation. Therefore, growth factors and cytokines directly activate Ras in the model</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">The Ras protein and Ras/ERK1/2 axis are  involved in many non-canonical biochemical signals</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14534577</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11057896</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Ras is a proto-oncogne, part of the GTPases familly. Ras proteins are activated after activation of a transmembranar receptor (mainly EGFR Epidermal Growth Factor receptor for the canonical pathway). Activation of Ras by phosphorylation leads to downstream cascade of Kinase activation (MEK/ERK), which contributes to transduce the signal up to the nucleus.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11057896</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">GTPases of the Ras superfamily</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11057896</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_1379a120-fc05-458d-82c9-aebacf6fe4be">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_8f8b86ce-160c-45ca-be5c-99a1011584b4" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_16" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="PTHrP">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Pathway Summary: PTHrP signals through PPR to activate PKA, Ca2+ transients and PKC pathways. Activation of the PTH/PTHrP receptor leads to activation of multiple G proteins. The activation of Gs leads to subsequent activation of adenylate cyclase and generation of cyclic AMP (cAMP). cAMP will then activate PKA, which will in turn phosphorylate Sox9, greatly increasing its activity.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16831900</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">10805756</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">1658941</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">PTHrP/PThrP receptor signalling breaks down hypertophy in chondrocytes and are involved in a Ihh/PthrP feedback loop controlling chondrocyte differentiation</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">9391087</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">8662546</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">PTHrP siganlling prevents premature chondrocyte hypertrophy notably by promoting Runx2 degradation. It also showed potential to inhibit hypertrophy in articular chondrocytes</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22971952</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19351720</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Pthlh, parathyroid hormone-like peptide, MGI:97800, NCBI gene:19227</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_8f8b86ce-160c-45ca-be5c-99a1011584b4">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="f3704362-ee2f-4518-b90b-bf5d33cedd29" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_17" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ATF4">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Activating transcription factor 4 (Atf4). Also known as AP-1 or CREB, MGI:88096, NCBI gene: 11911</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">ATF4 is a leucine-zipper-containing protein of the cAMP response element-binding protein (CREB) family. Ablation of Atf4 (Atf4−/−) in mice leads to severe skeletal defects, including delayed ossification and low bone mass, short stature and short limbs. Atf4 is expressed in proliferative and prehypertrophic growth plate and articular chondrocytes</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19906842</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#f3704362-ee2f-4518-b90b-bf5d33cedd29">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_05d64952-c1ff-48fb-b72c-f9ee73b83531" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_18" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="IGF-IR">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Insulin growth factor receptor (Igf1r): MGI:96433, NCBI gene:16001</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Insulin Growth Factor receptor</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_05d64952-c1ff-48fb-b72c-f9ee73b83531">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_0c7cc84c-938c-4816-88a3-854f3fee8dd8" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_19" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="STAT1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">STAT1 has a transcriptional activity in the nucleus in the form of homodimers</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26220993</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">signal transducer and activator of transcription-1 (Stat1) MGI:103063 , NCBI gene:20846</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">STAT1-p21 signalling mediates the inhibitory effects of FGFR3 on endochondral ossification &amp; in pathological conditions</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12960068</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12080084</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19088846</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11493533</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">STAT proteins are activated by phosphorylation thanks to JAK kinase. Intermediary JAK are not represented in the model for reason of simplification. The Stat1 in the model represents JAK/Stat1 pathway.</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19319849</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Stat1 is upregulated in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14962955</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">STAT1, originally identified as a signal transducing molecule in the interferon (IFN) pathway, is activated by tyrosine phosphorylation to dimerize and translocate into the nucleus where it then functions as a transcriptional regulator</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11493533</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_0c7cc84c-938c-4816-88a3-854f3fee8dd8">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-15T15:43:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_525c6716-f653-4b41-b9fa-31ff4cc9372f" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_20" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="TAK1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">TGF-β-activating kinase (TAK1 or Map3k7). MGI:1346877. NCBI Gene: 26409</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">After binding to membrane receptors (e.g. Toll-like receptors for Il-B), a kinases phosphorylation cascade takes place involving IRAK and TGF-β-activating kinase (TAK1), this linear transduction is summarized under the node TAK1 in the model  Downstream of TAK1, pro-inflammatory  stimuli are transduced via different pathways involving p38, ERK1/2, JNK and  Nf-kB</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17947700</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Pro-inflammatory cytokine pathways converge towards the activaction of TAK1 (via IRAK and other intermediraies not represented here)</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17947700</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20086235</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">TAK1 is activated by cascades of ubiquitinationa and phosphorylation</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20086235</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Inhibition of TAK1 prevents inflamamtion related cartilage degradation</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27682596</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Tak1 is essential for articular cartilage, notably because it is able to regulate SOX9 expression</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24144697</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_525c6716-f653-4b41-b9fa-31ff4cc9372f">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_9adf5ea8-e83d-4527-9e06-6d85285e6559" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_21" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="Infl. cytokines">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Pro-inflammatory cytokines upregulate catabolic processes, ECM degrading enzymes (MMPs) and other pro-inflammatory mediator, which constitutes a feedforward loop</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11714393</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21788902</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Pro-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1β (IL-1B) or TNF-α (TNF-alpha): Il1b MGI:96543, NCBI Gene: 16176</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Several pro-inflammatory cytokines can be senses by chondrocytes and activate downstream pathways, this node represent typical cytokines such as IL-1 aof TNF-alpha with generic pro-inflammatory pathways without making distinctions for the subtility of each kind of pro-inflammatory cytokines</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21788902</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20086235</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Even though osteoarthrtis is not a typical inflammatory disease (low synovitis grade), several pro-inflammatory pathways are overactivated in that disease and play an important role cartilage physiology</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21788902</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Il1-beta pathway activation is associated with chondrocyte phenotypic shift and osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17640966</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_9adf5ea8-e83d-4527-9e06-6d85285e6559">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="ab82e923-e511-4f2d-a507-eb54f7ca0033" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_22" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="MSX2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">mesh-less homeobox 2 (Msx2) Synonym: hox8. MGI:97169, NCBI gene:17702</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Variation of Msx2 expression was shown to be associated with osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20060954</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">mesh-less 2 (Msx2) is expressed in resting and proliferating chondrocytes. Msx2−/− mice show, in addition to other phenotypes, defects in both endochondral and intramembranous ossification</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">10742104</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Msx2 is an homeobox protein. Homeobox proteins are transcription factors that direct proper development by regulating specific temporal and spatial patterns of gene expression</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#ab82e923-e511-4f2d-a507-eb54f7ca0033">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_777a59d5-5819-409c-ae92-f49ef61d4d61" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_23" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="AKT">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Homozygous Akt1‐deficient (Akt1−/−) mice and Akt2−/− mice show similar phenotypes, including bone growth retardation</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20187155</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Phosphoinositide‐dependent serine/threonine protein kinase Akt, also called protein kinase B (PKB)</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Akt1 MGI:87986  NCBI Gene: 11651. Akt1 is the dominant form (among Akt1, Akt2 and Akt3) in skeletal development &amp; in chondrocyte</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20187155</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">The Pi3K/Akt axis integrates signals from pro-catabolic and pro-anabolic stimuli and regulates seemingly opposite functions</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25659655</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_777a59d5-5819-409c-ae92-f49ef61d4d61">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T13:14:57Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_2f16618d-3553-42f8-9cab-20bda58009c0" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_24" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="SMAD7">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Inhibitory Smad. MGI:1100518, NCBI gene:17131</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">inhibitory Smads are Smad6 and Smad7. Smad7 inhbits BMPs and TGF-βs signal in multiple ways: preventing R-Smad nuclei translocation, competitively binding with type I receptor to prevent R-Smad phosphorylation, and promoting R-smads or receptor degradation by recruiting E3 ubiquitin ligases Smurf1/2</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27563484</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Conditional-overexpression of Smad7 in MSC or stage-specific chondrocytes results in defective mesenchymal condensation, chondrocyte proliferation and chondrocyte maturation, respectively</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18644788</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">TGF-β superfamily ligands can induce expression of the I-Smads, suggesting that these Smads act as part of an autocrine negative feedback loop</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15694398</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Smad7 inhibits endochondral ossification in part by downregulating p38/MAPK pathway</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23994637</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18644788</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_2f16618d-3553-42f8-9cab-20bda58009c0">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:27:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="fe56a8c6-edfa-4ede-923f-a96c562e4183" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_25" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="SOX9">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">DNA-binding protein. It is one of the major transcription factor involved during chondrocyte differentiation.  Deficiencies lead to the skeletal malformation syndrome campomelic dysplasia, frequently with sex reversal</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31382142</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29308531</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">SRY (sex determining region Y)-box 9 (Sox9) MGI:98371, NCBI gene: 20682</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">High Mobility Group-box or SRY-box transcription factor</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">The negative feedback loop between Runx2 and Sox9 is crucial for the control of chondroycte stability and hypertophic differenitation, during development and disease</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29308531</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#fe56a8c6-edfa-4ede-923f-a96c562e4183">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:31:38Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_207cb18b-aca2-468a-bff1-1cb04b91310f" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_26" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="IGFI">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Insulin-like Growth Factor 1, IGF1 is involved in metabolism regulation and is very important for growth during development.</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (Igf1, IGF-I). MGI:96432, NCBI gene:16000</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">There is a complex and cell stage-specific anabolic action of IGF-I in the growth plate</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17446238</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">In articular chondrocytes, IGF-I regulates differentiation, maintenance of the differentiated phenotype, and apoptosis via a PI-3 kinase pathway that modulates ERK, p38 kinase, and PKC signaling</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12853454</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">IGF-I is know is able to promote cartilage matrix production. Even though it was reported ot be overexpressed in osteoarthritis (OA), its bio-availability at the protein level is curtailed by other factors during OA</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">9562333</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">7510486</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">IGF-I signalling is an important driver of cartilage anabolism and, disturbance of this pathway could be related to osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29131268</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_207cb18b-aca2-468a-bff1-1cb04b91310f">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_2fea815c-73be-4d6f-9b88-64bf5163c922" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_27" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="FGF">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">FGF2 was identified as a degenerative mediator (promote catabolism) in Osteoarthritis, although its exact role is controversial</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21996043</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21835001</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">FGFs 2, 9 and in particular FGF18 together with the receptors FGFR1 and 3 are implicated in growth plate development.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15863035</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17169623</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25772309</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26220993</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Via receptor activation, FGF ligands signal through various transduction pathways such as Ras/Raf/MEK/MAPK, PI3k/Akt, STAT1,</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20067622</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Fribroblast Growth factor ligands e.g. fgf18  (shown to bind fgfr3 and promote chondrogenisis). MGI:1277980 , NCBI gene:14172</p><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Fribroblast Growth factor signalling is essential for skeletal development as was first put in evidence with FGFR mutation associated bone morphongenic disorder such as achondroplasia (dwarfism)</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28298517</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Different FGF ligands have been shown to preferientially bind to different receptors and activate differential responses. For sake of simplification, only a global FGF node was introduce in the current model, however, making the dinstinction between FGF2 and FGF18 ligands, for example, might be important and subject to future imrpovments.</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11937493</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23060229</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_6" contentType="Description">The pro-anabolic or pro-catabolic nature of FGF ligands depends on the receptors mediating the signal, blocking on type of receptor has emerged as potential therapeutic strategy to tip the role of FGF signaling during OA</p><ul key="P_description_6">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33285208</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_7" contentType="Description">Using recombinant FGF18 migh be good therapeutic strategy against osteoarthritis as was demonstrated by several clinical trials</p><ul key="P_description_7">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24740822</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_2fea815c-73be-4d6f-9b88-64bf5163c922">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="d5649f09-f6fb-4989-a5be-f34e65c480c7" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_28" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ALK1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Activin A receptor, type II-like 1 (Acvrl1) (TβRII) MGI:1338946, NCBI Gene: 11482</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Receptors of the TGF super familly, they normally function in dimers and may be activated both by TGF-beta of BMPs</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Upon ligand binding, they tranduces signal via phosphorylation of downstream proteins, such as, Smads. They would promote rather pro-catabolic pathways and an increase of ALK1 amount and expression was obserged in ageing population and OA.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28148919</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21638205</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24928920</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#d5649f09-f6fb-4989-a5be-f34e65c480c7">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T10:45:38Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_369580d9-d7b0-4be3-a0a3-d00f10e6c927" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_29" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="SMAD3">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">The TGF-beta/Smad3 axis promotes pro-anabolic signals and tend to prevent chondrocyte hypertrophy. One of the mode of action might the recruitment of HDAC4 deacetylase to limit Runx2 transcriptional activity</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15990875</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11285272</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14534577</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19734454</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Smad3: MGI:1201674, NCBI gene: 17127</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Smad3/Smad2 transduce TGFβ signal in chondrocyte. In presence of TGFβ, Smad3 complexes with co-Smad4 and translocate in the nucleus where it has a transcriptional activity</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27741240</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20804388</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Smad3 might be involved in both onset and progression of OA. Smad3−/− mice gradually developed an end-stage OA phenotype</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Smad2 and Smad3 are considered to have similar funcitons in a number of context including cartilage and were represented as a single node in the model for sake of simplification</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27741240</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Smad2 and Smad3 interact with a variety of DNA-binding proteins (e.g. HDAC4) in different contexts, thereby modulation the activity of other transcriptional regulators</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_369580d9-d7b0-4be3-a0a3-d00f10e6c927">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-07T18:38:12Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="aef7407c-be6b-4d82-854d-f6e375453de3" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_30" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="SOCS">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">They also negatively regulate the JAK/STAT pathway in cartilage and during osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19319849</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28245561</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Suppressors of cytokine signaling -1 (Socs1). MGI:1354910, NCBI Gene: 12703</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">SOCS are soluble inhibitors of  pro-inflammatory cytokines receptors, they prevent the downstream phosphorylation cascades.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11553846</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18950705</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Socs prevent Nfkb activation by pro-inflammatory signals</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31319599</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#aef7407c-be6b-4d82-854d-f6e375453de3">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="b7a2ae13-c2ef-40bf-bc06-ad5976f5feec" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_31" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ERK1/2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">It is a signaling kinase protein transducing signals in the MAPK signalling pathways. It stands at the end of the cascade to phosphorylate and thereby activate various transcription factors.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14597193</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11057896</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">"ERK function in cartilage is to stimulate not cell proliferation, but rather chondrocyte maturation"</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17967876</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">mitogen-activated protein kinase or extracellular signal-regulated kinase1&amp;2.  ERK1 (Mapk3) MGI:1346859, NCBI gene: 26417 /  ERK2 (Mapk1): MGI:1346858, NCBI gene: 26413</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">extracellular signal-regulated kinase. ERK1/2 = MAPK3/1</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#b7a2ae13-c2ef-40bf-bc06-ad5976f5feec">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T13:14:57Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_18d79199-7b79-4aec-b3ae-bc111feca2db" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_32" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="IkBa">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Nuclear factor of kappa light polypeptide gene enhancer in B cells inhibitor, alpha (I(Kappa)B(alpha) or Nfkbi). MGI:104741, NCBI Gene: 18035</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">IκBα is a specific inhibitor of Nfkb, it  binds and sequestrate Nfkb proteins in the cytosol as well as promote its translocation outside the nucelus thereby preventing its transcriptional activity</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31319599</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Ikba is also inlvonved in an autoregulatory loop with Nfkb (see GRN)</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20086235</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">8096091</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_18d79199-7b79-4aec-b3ae-bc111feca2db">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_961c7750-2628-41a3-81f1-adb068efd7b3" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_33" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="P38">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">p38 or mitogen-activated protein kinase 14 (Mapk14) MGI:1346865, NCBI gene: 26416</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Phosphorylation of p38 is higher in human OA compared to normal tissue and p38 inhibitor was investigated as potential OA treatment</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19055838</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">p38 are mammalian MAPKs activated by the upstream kinases MKK3 and MKK6. p38 phosphorylation is increased during chondrogenisis and inhibition of p38activity blocks chondrogenisis</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14594450</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14597193</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">10681543</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">It may be involved in the control of the Sox9 function during chondrogensis via the BMP‐2/p38 pathway.</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18506848</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">p38 has important effects on chondroycte maturation and may be o potential target for OA therapies but its exact role remains controversial as it is located at the crosstalk of several pathways</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18975318</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33188182</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_961c7750-2628-41a3-81f1-adb068efd7b3">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_321884ec-cf9d-496c-8f1a-9203e8363b13" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_34" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="NFkB">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">NfkB is central in the intracellular transduction on pro-inflammatory signals.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21351054</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Nuclear Factor Kappa B Subunit (NFkb1 or p50: MGI:97312 , NCBI gene:18033) / (Rela/p65 : NCBI: 19697)</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">NF-κB regulates the expression of many cytokines and chemokines, adhesion molecules, inflammatory mediators, and several matrix degrading enzymes</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21351054</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20199390</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31319599</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">NfkB is involved in the pathophysiology of osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26557379</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">NF-kappa-B is a homo- or heterodimeric complex formed by the Rel-like domain-containing proteins RELA/p65, RELB, NFKB1/p105, NFKB1/p50, REL and NFKB2/p52</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20199390</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_321884ec-cf9d-496c-8f1a-9203e8363b13">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="eeab705a-2f0f-44d8-8f7f-4d4f8f26ebea" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_35" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="RUNX2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">runt related transcription factor 2 (Runx2) MGI:99829, NCBI gene: 12393</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Runx2 is a master transcription factor for hypertophy during endochondral ossification and it may play a important role in the development of osteoarthritis too</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28539595</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29308531</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Runt-related transcription factor 2; Transcription factor involved in osteoblastic differentiation and skeletal morphogenesis. Essential for both intramembranous and endochondral ossification.</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Also called Cbfa1 , PEBP2A or Osf2. Disease association database: OMIM600211</p><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">The negative feedback loop between Runx2 and Sox9 is crucial for the control of chondroycte stability and hypertophic differenitation, during development and disease</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29308531</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#eeab705a-2f0f-44d8-8f7f-4d4f8f26ebea">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_09235bb6-33ff-4d98-aa96-ace9e82aba87" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_36" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="Destruction complex">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">The destruction complex (DC) is a complex of which GSK3beta  in an important component (MGI:1861437), other molecules are also involved (Axin, APC, PP2A...)</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23169527</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">When the destruction complex is active,  B-cat is degraded and Wnt downstream targets are not expressed</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17143292</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21911068</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">When the wnt pathway is off, the transcriptional coactivator β-catenin is degraded by a multiprotein "destruction complex" that includes the tumor suppressors Axin and adenomatous polyposis coli (APC), the Ser/Thr kinases GSK-3 and CK1, protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A), and the E3-ubiquitin ligase β-TrCP.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17143292</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12615961</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23169527</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_09235bb6-33ff-4d98-aa96-ace9e82aba87">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_3f1a67ef-70f5-48a1-a46d-b23f5b13c52c" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_37" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="DLX5">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Homeobox transcription factor similar to Drosophila distal-less, important role in bone development and healing. NB: Homeobox proteins are transcription factors that direct proper development by regulating specific temporal and spatial patterns of gene expression.</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Dlx5 is expressed in (pre)hypertrophic chondrocyte and has a transcription activity promoting hypertrophy. Notably it would directly bind and activate Runx2 promotor.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19956613</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16901655</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18546336</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12895028</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12482714</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">distal-less homeobox 5: MGI:101926, NCBI gene:13395</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_3f1a67ef-70f5-48a1-a46d-b23f5b13c52c">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="db229200-7d5f-451c-b54b-091dfdeb589d" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_38" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="PKA">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">cAMP/protein kinase A (Prkaca), MGI:97592, NCBI gene: 18747</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">cAMP dependent protein kinase (PKA) is involed in PTHrP signalling, it tranduces the signal by phosphorylation of the downstream targets</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22971952</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Pharmacological inhibition of PKA blocks the chondrogenic differentiation of limb bud micromass cultures</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11092400</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25715393</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#db229200-7d5f-451c-b54b-091dfdeb589d">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_8a085e23-9ac3-41d7-9c8a-2de5ccf99deb" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_39" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="ALK5">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Transduces pro-anabolic signals upon TGFbeta ligands binding, and shows opposite effect with ALK1</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18333754</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">ALK5 or  transforming growth factor, beta receptor I (TβRI). Tgfbr1 MGI:98728, NCBI Gene: 21812</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">The ration ALK5/ALK1 decreases with ageing and osteoarthritis(OA). ALK5  downstream signalling would have a protective role against OA.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27563484</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17391995</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24928920</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28148919</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21638205</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Loss of ALK5 induces osteoarthritis in mouse</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28716756</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">The node ALK5 represents the effective heterodimer of type II receptors and the ALK5  type I receptor</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28148919</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_8a085e23-9ac3-41d7-9c8a-2de5ccf99deb">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-07T18:38:12Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="d8c87e84-724c-411c-88ed-032d04d6481a" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_40" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="MMP13">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Matrix metalloproteinase13 mmp1, collagenase-3: MGI:1340026, NCBI gene: 17386</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">MMP13 is a matrix degrading enzyme typically secreted by hypertrophic chondrocytes. It would be one of the major actor of matrix degradation during endochondral ossification but also osteoarthritis.</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">MMPs are produced by chondrocyte after pro-inflammatory stimuli</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16146751</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11714393</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">MMPs are the effectors of cartilage degradation during osteoarthritis but first attempt to inhibit them by direct drug targetting was proven inefficient to prevent OA</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17305507</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#d8c87e84-724c-411c-88ed-032d04d6481a">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T18:06:43Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_9987926f-9a09-4a69-8147-1bcaa969fe51" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_41" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="GREM1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Grem1 has a BMP binding kinase activity, it functions has an antagonist to BMP signalling</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24286177</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27769098</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Gremlin1 (Grem1): MGI:1344337, NCBI gene: 23892</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Gremlin mRNA level correlates with osteoarthritis (strongly decreased) and is regulated by osteoarthritis-associated factors. It is a natural brakes on hypertrophic differentiation in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24286177</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_9987926f-9a09-4a69-8147-1bcaa969fe51">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_0554888e-b6f9-469f-8a32-cb25a8ccb1b5" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_42" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="HIF-2a">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">HIF2α is a catabolic transcription factor with a likely role in the osteoarthritic process. HIF2α can become activated by pro-inflammatory cytokines and stress in the avascular, hypoxic environment of articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20495569</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20495570</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20526316</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21351054</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Hif-2alpha (hypoxic inducible factor) coded by the gene Epas1, MGI:109169 , NCBI gene:13819</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Hif-2alpha is induced in cartilage during hypoxia and HIF-2alpha enhanced promoter activities of COL10A1, MMP13 and VEGFA through specific binding to the respective hypoxia-responsive elements</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Hif2-alpha was associated with knee osteoarthritis in genetic population study, alhtough this association was not supported by various ethnic population study as reported in (M.Nakajima et al.)</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20592745</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20526316</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21217668</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_0554888e-b6f9-469f-8a32-cb25a8ccb1b5">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="dba29ada-7a2f-43e4-b03f-b96820a53e78" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_43" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="IHH">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Ihh is involved in a major feedback loop with PthrP to regulate chondrocyte differentiation in the growth plate. Ihh stimulates the production of PTHrP and thereby delays chondrocyte hypertrophy. Overall it is major regulator on chondrocyte phenotype</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12748651</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">11277077</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16187326</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">8662546</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Indian hedgehog (Ihh) MGI:96533, NCBI gene:16147</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Hedgehog (Hh) signalling pathway has critical function in bone formation. In particular Indian Hedgehog (Ihh) has an essential function in endochondral ossification</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26023726</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">IHH-PTHrP would also participate in the maintenance of articular cartilage. Dysregulation of this system might contribute to the pathogenesis of arthritis.</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19035497</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Ihh is normally upregulated togehter with Runx2 in (pre)hypertophic chondrocytes</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16187326</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_5" contentType="Description">Modulating Ihh signalling would be a therapeurical strategy for osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_5">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19915594</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#dba29ada-7a2f-43e4-b03f-b96820a53e78">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_2fa825e8-da50-4a71-817a-02c3e19fe39d" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_44" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="SMAD-complex">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">It is part of the Bmp canonical pathway but it is also regulated by TGB-beta in articular chondrocytes</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22172041</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Smad-complex denotes a complex formed of R-Smads and Smad4. The resulting complex has a transcriptional activity in the nucleus.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16322555</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Smad4: MGI:894293, NCBI gene: 17128</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_2fa825e8-da50-4a71-817a-02c3e19fe39d">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_362eb999-5860-469f-92b9-78fbaca734a1" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_45" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="LEF/TCF">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">When the Wnt pathway is activated LEF/TCF direclty bind to the Runx2 promotor to activate Runx2 transcription.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16575901</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">LEF and TCF7 are transcription factors downstream of Wnt pathway. They regulate the experssion of Wnt targets.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23024173</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22550232</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Tcf7:  MGI:98507; ncbi gene: 21414</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_362eb999-5860-469f-92b9-78fbaca734a1">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_964dc05b-3b21-4b73-9a0d-ffd2317f5213" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_46" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="FOXO">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Forkhead Box O. E.g. Foxo1 MGI:1890077, NCBI Gene: 56458</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">"In OA chondrocytes, overexpression of FoxO1 reduced inflammatory mediators and cartilage-degrading enzymes, increased protective genes, and antagonized interleukin-1β effects"</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29444976</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">FOXO synergize with the TGF-beta pathway to regulate to downstream targets</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29444976</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Deletion of Foxo showed that it is involved in expression of Col2a1 and Acan in cartilage</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31601652</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">FOXO is involved in the regulation of several ECM component and the deletion of Foxo resulted in spontenous degradation of articular cartilage.</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29444976</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_964dc05b-3b21-4b73-9a0d-ffd2317f5213">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T13:14:57Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_9c11c48e-048d-4197-a170-ae153aee2067" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_47" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="B-catenin">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Chondrocyte-specific B-catenin deletion results in decreased proliferation and delayed hypertrophic differentiation</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19557172</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20097766</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">β-catenin was upregulated in articular cartilage during osteoarthritis.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18767925</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22172041</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Beta-catenin is an intracellular transducer of canonical Wnt pathway.</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Ctnnb1. MGI:88276, NCBI gene:12387</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_9c11c48e-048d-4197-a170-ae153aee2067">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="af42b7d8-d697-46bf-90e2-0893f42f18f3" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_48" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="d-EF1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">[delta]-EF1 or Zeb1. MGI:1344313, NCBI gene:: 21417</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">This gene encodes a zinc finger transcription factor. It can act as positive or negative transcriptional regulator</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Zeb1 is expressed in cartiage and is increased in osteoarthritic patients, it may be contribute to chondrocyte hypertophy and OA.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31860959</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">d-EF1 (or Zeb1) play a central role in the decrease of collagen II and cartilage matrix production</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12133909</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#af42b7d8-d697-46bf-90e2-0893f42f18f3">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="c1949fe9-21b2-4292-aaa4-fb717566c42a" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_49" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="R-Smad">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">receptor-activated Smads (R-smad) are activated when BMP binds to its membranar receptor and they associate with Smad4 to form the Smad-complex, which has a transcriptional activity in the nucleus and transduces BMP signal.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14687659</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17886256</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16215986</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Smad1/5/9. E.g. Smad5: MGI:1328787, NCBI gene:17129</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">R-smads are Smad1/5/8 in human and Smad1/5/9 in mouse.</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#c1949fe9-21b2-4292-aaa4-fb717566c42a">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T10:44:36Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_59620c92-871e-416d-95a7-fb7d0346f2de" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_50" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="WNT">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">WNTs are growth factors that play a vital role in proliferation and hypertrophy of chondrocytes. There are essential for joint health.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">29021569</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19557172</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15866164</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Wnt pathway summary: The pathway is initiated bythe  binding of WNT ligands to the WNT receptor (frizzled)/co-receptor (lipoprotein receptor-related protein-LRP-5/6) complex, triggering a conformational change in the downstream molecule complex that consist of Dishevelled, adenomatosis polyposis coli (APC), axin, glycogen synthase kinase 3β (GSK3β), β-catenin, and other proteins. Through sequential changes in interaction and phosphorylation status of these proteins, phosphorylation of the amino-terminal domain of β-catenin is disturbed, leading to stabilization and nuclear translocation of β-catenin. The nuclear-translocated β-catenin stimulates transcription of the target genes with co-transcription factors such as T-cell factor/lymphoid-enhancing factor (TCF/LEF) family</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26641070</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Wingless-type MMTV integration site family, several types exist; e.g. Wnt3a, MGI:98955, NCBI gene: 22416</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">WNT promotes hypertrophy through activation of Runx2 transcription factor</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16575901</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">Wnt is reported as a risk factor in Osteoarthritis</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26641070</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19136083</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_59620c92-871e-416d-95a7-fb7d0346f2de">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:36:56Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_25cc7a17-66ac-42a8-bcff-980feb51dff2" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_51" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="NKX3.2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">NK3 homeobox 2 (nkx3-2), also called Bapx1: MGI:108015, NCBI gene: 12020</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Nkx3.2 is an homeobox protein, therefore it has a transcriptional activity. It is strongly involved int he process of endochondral ossification. It notably act concomitantly with Sox9 to repress the expression of Runx2. It is a negative regulator of chondrocyte maturation</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27158253</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">16421188</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Overexpression of Nkx3.2 promotes and inhibition of Nkx3.2 suppresses chondrogenic differentiation. It promotes Col-II and GAG epxression.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27158253</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19306868</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22511961</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_25cc7a17-66ac-42a8-bcff-980feb51dff2">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_001cac40-ff0e-4714-9f1b-21e3228efd63" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_52" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="GSK3beta">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Gsk3b, MGI:1861437, NCBI gene: 56637</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">This node is a proxy for free GSK3beta (= GSK3B not included in Destruction complex). So it represents the activity of GSK3beta in other signalling parhway than the Wnt/Bcat one.</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12615961</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta is  is a multifunctional serine/threonine kinase found in all eukaryotes. It is involved in several cellular signalling pathways where it controls activity of downtream factors including transcription factors</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12615961</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_001cac40-ff0e-4714-9f1b-21e3228efd63">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_04f77f15-3f8a-4faa-bb1c-84f57bb566b8" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_53" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="PP2A">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Protein phosphatase 2 (formerly 2A) (Ppp2ca) MGI:1321159, NCBI gene: 19052</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Direct messenger of PKA downstream of the PTHrP growth factor. It is a negative regulator of ERK and AKT</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25715393</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">It is involved in the regulation of several important regulators of chondrocyte maturation (HDAC4, AKT, ERK1/2, etc..) and also involved in the formation of the destruction complex within hte Wnt/beta-catenin pathway</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22971952</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23169527</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_04f77f15-3f8a-4faa-bb1c-84f57bb566b8">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_2ae11629-4a17-4cda-87ca-3f1a23309626" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_54" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="JNK">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">It is a signaling kinase protein transducing signals in the MAPK signalling pathways together with ERK1/3 and p38. It stands at the end of the cascade to phosphorylate and thereby activate various transcription factors.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14597193</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) or mitogen-activated protein kinase 8 (Mapk8). MGI:1346861. NCBI Gene: 26419</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">It is involved in the transduction of pro-inflammatory signals and the downstream transcriptional effectors represented in the model are Elk1/Ets and ATF2</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33188182</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_2ae11629-4a17-4cda-87ca-3f1a23309626">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T13:14:57Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="a1839499-0b86-4af5-a25e-2e3c212cb0af" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_55" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="Frizzled-LRP5/6">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">LRP5: MGI:1278315  NCBI Gene: 16973. / LRP6: MGI:1298218  NCBI Gene: 16974.</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">The Wnt pathway is activated by binding of WNT to Frizzled family receptors</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Frizzled-LRP5/6 represents the (frizzled)/co-receptor (lipoprotein receptor-related protein-LRP-5/6) complex</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">seven-pass transmembrane receptor Frizzled (FZD)</p><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">single-pass low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 5 or 6 (LRP5/6</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#a1839499-0b86-4af5-a25e-2e3c212cb0af">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_41cf9b81-0357-4230-87c7-ba287a03259b" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_56" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="HDAC4">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Overexpression of HDAC4 in proliferating chondrocytes, in vivo, inhibits  hypertrophy and differentiation.</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25715393</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15537544</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Histone deacetylases (HDACs) modulate cell growth and differentiation by governing chromatin structure and repressing the activity of specific transcription factors. HDAC4 affects transcription fators access to DNA</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">15537544</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24579951</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">Histone deacetylase 4: MGI:3036234, NCBI gene:208727</p><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">"Acetylation of lysine residues within histone protein tails reduces their affinity for DNA, thereby relaxing the chromatin structure and increasing the accessibility of DNA to transcription factor. [...] Histone deacetylases (HDACs) are capable of reversing this process by removing the acetyl moiety and, therefore, allowing histones to bind tightly to DNA again"</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19734454</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_41cf9b81-0357-4230-87c7-ba287a03259b">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_0f4d435c-593e-4b18-ad09-8a787181e4d1" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_57" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="PI3K">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (pi3kr1). MGI:97583, NCBI gene: 18708</p><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">PI3K pathway control hypetrophic differentiation during endochondral ossification.  PI3K inhibition reduces the length of the proliferating and particularly of the hypertrophic zone</p><ul key="P_description_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18405384</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">the Pi3k/Akt axis lies at the crossroad of several pathways hence it potential dual role, depending on the cellular context and environement. For instance, pro-inflammatoiry cytokines, produced during OA, are able to activate that axis.</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">32189997</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28320092</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12853454</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">The Pi3K/Akt axis integrates signals from pro-catabolic and pro-anabolic stimuli and regulates seemingly opposite functions</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25659655</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_0f4d435c-593e-4b18-ad09-8a787181e4d1">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-08T10:59:15Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="fb631cd9-d06f-469a-b3f6-1610ab2cb42f" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_58" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="CCND1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">Cyclin D1.  Cyclin dependent kinase (CDK) are proteins promoting cell cylce. Progression through the G1 phase and the G1/S transition check point is achieved by the activity of CDKs and negatively regulated by CDK inhibitors (e.g. p21 in cartilage)</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">9832503</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">cyclin D1 (Ccnd1) MGI:88313, NCBI gene: 12443</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#fb631cd9-d06f-469a-b3f6-1610ab2cb42f">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2019-02-05T04:22:17Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="cb01e09a-d18c-438e-9419-c639642fb895" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="true" qual:id="S_59" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="TGF-beta">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">TGF-beta plays a key role in chondrocyte hypertophy and cartilage homeostasis. Modification of this pathway may lead to osteoarthritis and it is claimed to be a potential way of action for candidate drugs. Downstream effects depend on the ALK1/ALK5 ratio, which changes with ageing and in OA</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28148919</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">24928920</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">23685840</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">21638205</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17391995</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">25475114</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">Transforming growth factor, beta (e.g. Tgfb1): MGI:98725, NCBI gene: 21803</p><p key="P_description_2" contentType="Description">TGF-β superfamily members act through a heteromeric receptor complex, comprised of type I and type II receptors (TβRI &amp; TβRII) at the cell surface that transduce intracellular signals via Smad3, Smad complex or the non-canonical-Smad-independent signaling pathway (that is, p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase/p38 MAPK)</p><ul key="P_description_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27848974</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27563484</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_3" contentType="Description">Mice expressing a dominant negative TβRII exhibit articular cartilage degeneration similar to that observed in human OA. Abnormal expression of type X collagen is found in the articular cartilage of these mice</p><ul key="P_description_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">9334355</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_4" contentType="Description">By binding to different receptors of the activin-receptor like kinase (ALK) family, TGFβ induces intracellular carboxy-terminal (C-terminal) phosphorylation of receptor-regulated Smad (R-Smad) proteins. The type of activated receptor will define which R-smad are activated knowing that in cartilage, phosphorylated Smad2/3 and Smad1/5 have opposing functions regarding stable chondrocytes maintenance</p><ul key="P_description_4">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">28569204</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#cb01e09a-d18c-438e-9419-c639642fb895">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-07T18:38:12Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
      <qual:qualitativeSpecies metaid="_59f8b3ca-99a3-41f6-b252-44503e520f57" qual:compartment="cytosol" qual:constant="false" qual:id="S_60" qual:initialLevel="0" qual:maxLevel="1" qual:name="DSH">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_description_0" contentType="Description">The Wnt signal signals through its receptor to activate Dishevelled (Dvl1)</p><ul key="P_description_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22550232</span></li></ul><p key="P_description_1" contentType="Description">MGI:98297, NCBI gene: 20423</p></body>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
	<rdf:Description rdf:about="#_59f8b3ca-99a3-41f6-b252-44503e520f57">
	<dcterms:modified rdf:parseType="Resource">
	<dcterms:W3CDTF>2021-02-05T09:24:02Z</dcterms:W3CDTF>
	</dcterms:modified>
	</rdf:Description>
	</rdf:RDF>
	</annotation>
            </qual:qualitativeSpecies>
    </qual:listOfQualitativeSpecies>
    <qual:listOfTransitions xmlns:qual="http://www.sbml.org/sbml/level3/version1/qual/version1">
      <qual:transition qual:id="tr_S_1">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_regulatorysummary_0" contentType="RegulatorySummary">When hh signalling is active a succession of steps, involving the cillium organelle, starts and eventually promotes the dissociation of the binding between Gli and Sufu, leading to Gli2/3 activation. Active forms of Gli2 relocate to the nucleus to activate the expression of Hh target genes. (canonical)</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">26023726</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">19666565</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <qual:listOfInputs>
          <qual:input qual:id="tr_S_1_in_S_43" qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_43" qual:sign="positive" qual:thresholdLevel="1" qual:transitionEffect="none"/>
        </qual:listOfInputs>
        <qual:listOfOutputs>
          <qual:output qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_1" qual:transitionEffect="assignmentLevel"/>
        </qual:listOfOutputs>
        <qual:listOfFunctionTerms>
          <qual:defaultTerm qual:resultLevel="0">
          </qual:defaultTerm>
          <qual:functionTerm qual:resultLevel="1">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">            
              <apply>
                <eq/>
                <ci> S_43 </ci>
                <cn type="integer"> 1 </cn>
              </apply>
            </math>
                    </qual:functionTerm>
        </qual:listOfFunctionTerms>
      </qual:transition>
      <qual:transition qual:id="tr_S_2">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_regulatorysummary_0" contentType="RegulatorySummary">Grem1 is an antognist of BMP, it inhibits it by binding, which subsequently brakes hypertrophic differentiation in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">27769098</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">22576962</span></li></ul><p key="P_regulatorysummary_1" contentType="RegulatorySummary">"BMPs transduce signals through complexes of type I and type II serine/threonine kinase receptors. Upon BMP binding, type II receptors phosphorylate serine/threonine residues in type I receptors. Activated type I receptors phosphorylate, and thereby activate, receptor-regulated SMAD (R-SMAD)1, R-SMAD5 and R-SMAD8. Subsequently, these R-SMADs recruit and bind SMAD4. These SMAD complexes enter the nucleus and regulate transcription of target genes"</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">14656483</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">17065231</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">31450621</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <qual:listOfInputs>
          <qual:input qual:id="tr_S_2_in_S_41" qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_41" qual:sign="negative" qual:thresholdLevel="1" qual:transitionEffect="none"/>
        </qual:listOfInputs>
        <qual:listOfOutputs>
          <qual:output qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_2" qual:transitionEffect="assignmentLevel"/>
        </qual:listOfOutputs>
        <qual:listOfFunctionTerms>
          <qual:defaultTerm qual:resultLevel="0">
          </qual:defaultTerm>
          <qual:functionTerm qual:resultLevel="1">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">            
              <apply>
                <not/>
                <apply>
                  <eq/>
                  <ci> S_41 </ci>
                  <cn type="integer"> 1 </cn>
                </apply>
              </apply>
            </math>
                    </qual:functionTerm>
        </qual:listOfFunctionTerms>
      </qual:transition>
      <qual:transition qual:id="tr_S_3">
        <notes>
          <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
            <p key="P_regulatorysummary_0" contentType="RegulatorySummary">p38 and Smad3 are different actors TFG-β signal transduction pathways which act synergistically to amplify TGF-β signal. Together they promote ATF2 transcriptional activity. Smad3 would form an heterocomplex with p38, which would prevent p38 from dephosphorylation and inhibition, hence promoting even more ATF2 transcriptional activation</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_0">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">10085140</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">20506210</span></li></ul><p key="P_regulatorysummary_1" contentType="RegulatorySummary">Smad3 and ATF2 cooperate to transduce TGFB effect on chondrocyte maturation</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_1">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">12878171</span></li></ul><p key="P_regulatorysummary_2" contentType="RegulatorySummary">ATF2 acts as a TF downstream of the MAPK/p38 pathway in articular cartilage</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_2">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul><p key="P_regulatorysummary_3" contentType="RegulatorySummary">ATF2 is aslo activated by the JNK kinase and activated downstream of inflammation pathway, those JNK/ATF2 specific effects are directly implemented at transcriptional level (see GRN)</p><ul key="P_regulatorysummary_3">
              <li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">33188182</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">7824938</span></li><li>
                <span class="referType">PMID</span><span class="position">0</span><span class="doi"/><span class="pmid">18698181</span></li></ul></body>
        </notes>
        <qual:listOfInputs>
          <qual:input qual:id="tr_S_3_in_S_33" qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_33" qual:sign="positive" qual:thresholdLevel="1" qual:transitionEffect="none"/>
          <qual:input qual:id="tr_S_3_in_S_29" qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_29" qual:sign="positive" qual:thresholdLevel="1" qual:transitionEffect="none"/>
        </qual:listOfInputs>
        <qual:listOfOutputs>
          <qual:output qual:qualitativeSpecies="S_3" qual:transitionEffect="assignmentLevel"/>
        </qual:listOfOutputs>
        <qual:listOfFunctionTerms>
          <qual:defaultTerm qual:resultLevel="0">
          </qual:defaultTerm>
          <qual:functionTerm qual:resultLevel="1">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">            
              <apply>
                <or/>
                <apply>
                  <or/>
                  <apply>
                    <and/>
                    <apply>
                      <eq/>
                      <ci> S_33 </ci>
                      <cn type="integer"> 1 </cn>
                    </apply>
                    <apply>
                      <eq/>
                      <ci> S_29 </ci>
                      <cn type="integer"> 1 </cn>
                    </apply>
                  </apply>
                  <apply>
                    <and/>
                    <apply>
                      <eq/>
